# Englische Projekttexte (britisches Englisch), Fachbegriffe gemäss GLOSSAR.md
# Pro Projekt: name, sub, text, desc (gleiche Anzahl Abschnitte wie deutsch: [Titel, Text])
PROJEKTE = {}
AREAL = {'Arealentwicklung Zürcherstrasse, Volketswil': 'Zürcherstrasse site development, Volketswil'}
def P(pid, **k): PROJEKTE[pid] = k

P('016-kpf', name='St. Katharina Bell Tower',
  sub='Acoustic cladding and lighting design for the bell tower',
  text='Acoustic cladding and lighting design for the existing bell tower of the Church of St. Catherine of Siena in Fällanden.')

P('180-mwu', name='Wilstrasse Apartment Building',
  sub='New apartment building with six owner-occupied apartments',
  text='New “Wilstrasse” apartment building with six owner-occupied apartments right next to the Aabach stream in Uster. Winner of the AFZO Building Award in 2003.',
  desc=[['', 'The building occupies a special site within a protected trio of trees – oak, birch and walnut – and along the green corridor of the Aabach, which runs from the town park to the newly created Hofuren green belt.'],
        ['', 'The design responds to these particular conditions through its number of storeys, its massing and its choice of materials. On the stream side, a front of windows and balconies extends along the entire length, drawing in the watercourse. Towards the street, the building’s “back” is clearly and strictly defined by vertical, rear-ventilated and dynamically curved Eternit cladding. Thanks to the large proportion of glazing, the owner-occupied apartments are flooded with light and open up to their surroundings. The structural system allows the floor plans to be divided almost at will with non-load-bearing walls.'],
        ['', 'The south-east and north-west elevations are expressed very differently. This stems from their orientation to the sun and from the different noise-protection requirements. On the sunny south-east elevation, the system made it possible to create a continuous, planar skin. On the street side, only narrow openings were punched out so as not to compromise the visual effect of the “noise shield”. The design concept, with a building grid of 64 cm based on the human stride, could be divided up with storey-high Eternit panels on the same module. Openings and solid “skin” complement each other rhythmically.']])

P('198-psu', name='Oberuster Primary School',
  sub='Conversion of the listed primary school',
  text='Oberuster Primary School was built in 1855 and gained an additional storey in 1902. It underwent extensive conversion in the 1950s. The building is on the municipal heritage inventory and was converted in line with heritage protection requirements.',
  desc=[['Heritage protection', 'Oberuster Primary School was built in 1855 and gained an additional storey in 1902. It underwent extensive conversion in the 1950s. The building is on the municipal heritage inventory and was converted in line with heritage protection requirements. No significant changes could be made to its external appearance. The necessary sun protection was installed with the utmost restraint in the form of fabric blinds. The central projection on the north side was redesigned at the entrance in a simple, clear form. Inside, the parquet floors and wood panelling in the classrooms were retained to meet heritage requirements.'],
        ['Brief', 'Until the conversion, the library and the staff room of the twelve-class school were housed provisionally in two apartments on the ground floor. Now a library meeting today’s requirements, with a book area and a media zone, has been created. In the attractively glazed anteroom, exhibitions and a play area spark the pupils’ interest. Next to the library are the head teacher’s office, a meeting room and two group rooms. The oversized classrooms of more than 90 m² were fitted with modern furnishings. Movable cupboards allow the rooms to be divided flexibly and make it possible to arrange group workspaces individually.'],
        ['Controlled ventilation', 'The school stands on the busy Aathalstrasse. To keep noise exposure as low as possible, and for energy reasons, all teaching rooms were fitted with controlled ventilation.']])

P('200-pks', name='Hegger Kindergarten',
  sub='Conversion of the kindergarten',
  text='The building houses three kindergartens, the school office and, in the basement, rooms for adult education courses.',
  desc=[['Brief', 'The building houses three kindergartens, the school office and, in the basement, rooms for adult education courses. The vertical organisation was shaped by the different times at which the various parts are used. The upper floor therefore accommodates two kindergartens and the school office, which has its own separate entrance. In the basement, besides the course rooms, there are the school archive and storerooms for the kindergartens and the caretaker. The existing kindergarten on the ground floor was gently renovated. The classrooms are linked to the group rooms, which lie at a lower level. The original building with its two dominant classrooms was integrated into the additional storey by means of strikingly coloured cubes.'],
        ['Daylight', 'A generous skylight above the stairwell brings plenty of daylight into the entrance hall. The upper classrooms are reached via the light-filled forecourt.'],
        ['Structure', 'Large spans of more than 9 metres were bridged elegantly with the prefabricated timber element construction chosen.']])

P('206-rbl', name='SBB Staff Restaurant',
  sub='New staff restaurant and seminar rooms, general planning commission',
  text='The site of the new staff restaurant between the tracks of the marshalling yard is extremely tight. Only by moving the access road by around 2 metres and staggering the building volume could the required brief be fitted into the site.',
  desc=[['', 'The site of the new staff restaurant between the tracks of the marshalling yard is extremely tight. Only by moving the access road by around 2 metres and staggering the building volume could the required brief be fitted into the site.'],
        ['', 'The building is added as an extension to the train drivers’ building completed only a year earlier, matching it in height and rhythm. The extension was designed according to quantitative and economic criteria. The ground floor houses the new restaurant with its dining room and infrastructure. A specially developed free-flow concept makes it easier for users to choose their meals. On the upper floor, offices and training and meeting rooms are accessed directly from the old building. The instruction rooms are needed to train 700 employees. Changing rooms and a lounge for visitors are reached via a separate covered external staircase.'],
        ['', 'The forecourt is raised to form a platform which, partly covered, links outside and inside. Bright red planter cubes separate work and rest. The nearby tracks with their locomotives are drawn into the design concept like a picture through wall-sized windows.']])

P('218-mfl', name='Lorenallee Apartment Building',
  sub='New free-standing building with 18 exclusive owner-occupied apartments',
  text='New elongated free-standing building with 18 exclusive owner-occupied apartments.',
  desc=[['', 'The transition from urban development to an ecological conservation area is deliberately designed. The building refers not only to itself, but also to the new neighbourhood taking shape around it. The residential block finds its spatial anchoring – its hold amid the tensions of the place – towards the urban side in its head building. Its form arose from shifting the brise-soleil towards the nature reserve.'],
        ['', 'The residential block on top has more volume than the plinth storey, which is masked by perforated metal sheets. The plinth is partly translucent and reveals the load-bearing structure. At night, artificial light projects this structural layering into the urban space, creating a mysterious, almost magical atmosphere.'],
        ['', 'The inner quality of the apartments lies in the flow of space through the hall into the living and dining room. The extra-tall windows are stepped back in depth and connect the interior with the outdoors.'],
        ['The unusual', 'One special feature: a drainage channel running across the plot ruled out a continuous underground car park. Instead, single and double garages were built at ground level, forming a translucent plinth beneath the residential building with a band of light. This band of light in yellow, blue and red gives each unit an exclusive, personal touch. The colour accents are discreetly picked up in the lift shafts. Each apartment can also be purchased together with a ground-level hobby room and its own section of garden.']])

P('235-bsw', name='SBB Railway Station', sub='Interior fit-out of the SBB ticket office and travel agency',
  text='Interior fit-out of the SBB ticket office and travel agency.',
  desc=[['Site', 'The ticket hall of Wallisellen SBB station is a single-storey volume beneath a larger, elongated building complex running parallel to the tracks, located centrally at the main underpass.'],
        ['Appearance', 'Towards the outside, the ticket hall opens up through generous floor-to-ceiling structural glazing and automatic sliding doors.'],
        ['Layout', 'Besides the ticket hall with its travel agency area, the brief includes a luggage store, a waiting area and toilets.'],
        ['Design', 'The building services – ventilation, chilled convectors, lights and parts of the electrical installations – are concealed in the suspended timber grid ceiling. As a design element, the grid ceiling also provides a counterpoint to the otherwise cool steel, glass and concrete architecture and, together with the yellow-orange wall colour, offers travellers a “homely” atmosphere.']])

P('241-mrn', name='Seegarten Housing', sub='New housing development with five apartment buildings in the village core zone',
  text='New housing development of five apartment buildings with controlled ventilation in the core zone of Niederuster, close to the Greifensee nature reserve.',
  desc=[['Development', 'The urban and architectural character of the neighbourhood is shaped primarily by the volumetric relationship between the buildings – a continuum of mass and space. The development engages with its existing context, and the density of the buildings matches the grain that has grown over time in the Niederuster core zone. Through the balance between buildings and outdoor space, the five apartment buildings form a coherent ensemble in their own right.'],
        ['Apartments', 'The position of each apartment within the overall arrangement determines the type of floor plan. The kitchen and the living/dining area are adapted to the situation in each case.'],
        ['Access', 'Pedestrians and cars are completely separated. All parking spaces are in the underground garage.'],
        ['Outdoor space', 'Paths and squares structure the space. Play and recreation areas are created inside the development. The new village square between the listed farmhouse and the housing development becomes the element linking old and new.'],
        ['Materials', 'Thanks to their solid construction and conceptual arrangement of windows, the buildings appear simple and unobtrusive. The roofs relate to the surrounding district.']])

P('245-anw', name='Convenience Shop', sub='New convenience shop in concrete and timber construction',
  text='New convenience shop in Niederweningen, a pavilion in combined concrete and timber construction with a rear-ventilated Scobalit façade.')

P('251-asch', name='Convenience Shop', sub='New pavilion and convenience shop in concrete and timber construction',
  text='New convenience shop, a pavilion in combined concrete and timber construction with a rear-ventilated glass façade, right next to the lake.',
  desc=[['', 'The high architectural ambitions were met with precise detailing. Using glass, concrete and steel, a clear concept responding to the immediate surroundings was realised. The lakeside setting is translated in stylised form across the tracks to the station square. The same façade reflects Schmerikon’s historic row of houses. The resulting image brings together these hallmarks of the place at the central transport hub. With a generously covered forecourt and bistro seating, the station does justice to its special location. Picturesque and technical elements combine to create an attractive meeting place which, thanks to its openness, also contributes to safety at the station.']])

P('254-mad', name='Alpenstrasse Apartment Building', sub='MINERGIE level with geothermal probe and controlled ventilation',
  text='Apartment building with four owner-occupied apartments built to MINERGIE level, with a geothermal probe and controlled ventilation.',
  desc=[['', 'The neighbourhood of detached houses near the station is increasingly being densified and mixed with apartment buildings. On a gently sloping hillside plot on the crest of a moraine ridge, four owner-occupied apartments were built. The irregularly angled building, with its main living orientation to the south-west, is accessed via the centrally located lift and the naturally lit staircase on the street façade. The building was constructed to the MINERGIE standard with a geothermal heat pump system and decentralised, controlled ventilation.']])

P('258-bpa', name='SBB Railway Station', sub='Conversion of a listed architectural icon from 1966',
  text='Conversion of the SBB sales and office premises in Max Vogt’s listed Zurich Altstetten station (built 1966).',
  desc=[['', 'Altstetten station from 1968 is so far the only building by the architect Max Vogt to be listed on a heritage inventory. The redesign of the interior and façade aims to respect Max Vogt’s design principles as closely as possible. Accommodating new uses placed high demands on the design. The layout follows an invisible grid set by Vogt. The hall’s original sense of space is preserved by glass elements reaching up to the ceiling. Its original size can still be experienced thanks to the continuation of the flat acoustic ceiling over the back-office workstations. The wood-panelled rear wall, a design element by Vogt, was reinstated in the same place.'],
        ['', 'The open travel agency and ticket sales desks can be opened independently of each other thanks to transparent, lockable partitions.'],
        ['', 'A “sunny” colour accent highlights the travel agency zone. The floor covering and ceiling structure were largely retained or recreated in their original character. The new lighting underlines the rigour of the architecture. The building’s energy performance was upgraded without affecting the protected fabric. By cutting out concrete elements in the façade, more daylight was brought into the rooms, some of which now have new functions. The new windows follow the principles of Vogt’s building, but in line with today’s possibilities they are larger.']])

P('261-10-swn', name='Wüeri School Complex', sub='Extension, conversion and refurbishment of the listed Wüeri school complex',
  text='Extension, conversion and refurbishment of the listed Wüeri school complex from 1966/1974, carried out in phases while the school remained in use.',
  desc=[['Brief', 'During the conversion and refurbishment of the school complex, barrier-free access for people with disabilities was improved, rooms were swapped, and wheelchair-accessible toilets and a stairlift were installed. Individual rooms such as the natural sciences room, the staff room, the music hall and the caretaker’s flat were modernised and brought up to today’s standards. Measures for fire safety and earthquake resistance were also necessary.'],
        ['Façade refurbishment', 'The façade was refurbished in close consultation with the cantonal heritage conservation office, as wings A and B are listed on the Canton of Zurich’s inventory of buildings worthy of protection. The external insulation, partly clad with ceramic tiles at the end of the 1980s, was removed. For energy reasons and to improve ventilation, the old windows in wings A and B were replaced with new ones.'],
        ['Materials and colour', 'In consultation with the cantonal heritage conservation office and on the basis of colour analyses, the exposed concrete façades of wings A and B from the 1960s and 1970s and the corridors of wing A were finished and painted in keeping with their original character. The corridors also received new energy-efficient lighting.'],
        ['Gymnasium', 'To improve ventilation in the two gymnasiums, automatic window-opening and shading systems were installed. The lower gymnasium received a barrier-free side entrance. At the same time, an accessible changing room and toilet were integrated into a covered side extension. This entrance also serves as an emergency exit for the lower gymnasium, so that the hall can now be used for events with larger numbers of people.'],
        ['ICT cabling', 'Following a uniform concept, the entire school complex was rewired and networked internally and fitted with Wi-Fi access points.']])

P('261-swn', name='Wüeri School Complex', sub='Conversion and refurbishment of wing B of the secondary school',
  text='Conversion and refurbishment of a lower secondary school building in Nänikon in three short construction phases during the school holidays.',
  desc=[['Brief', 'The conversion and refurbishment covered the corridors and toilets of wing B. In the corridors, all surfaces except the floor were refurbished and new fittings such as pinboards, display cases and coat racks were added. The toilets were completely stripped out and all pipework and sanitary fittings renewed.'],
        ['', 'Materials and colour: the corridor ceilings were clad with a perforated acoustic plasterboard ceiling whose homogeneity balances the restlessness of the polygonal floor plan. At the classroom entrances, the existing ceiling downstands (originally ventilation ducts) were used to emphasise the entrances with a colour accent. The colour of the entrance niches changes from floor to floor.'],
        ['Cloakroom', 'Along the classrooms, a strip combining pinboard and coat rack was mounted on the wall. The pinboard cover of perforated metal with a cork backing can be used to display pupils’ work with magnets, pins or tape.'],
        ['Lighting design', 'Lighting design played a particularly important role in the redesign of the school. In the corridors, the existing ceiling openings between the basement and the ground floor and between the first and second floors were enhanced with so-called “artificial skylights”: a luminous ceiling was mounted within a ceiling downstand across the entire opening (2.50 × 2.50 m). For the light colour of the luminous ceiling, daylight white was chosen as a deliberate contrast to the warm white of the other lights, to strengthen the impression of natural light falling in.']])

P('263-piu', name='Im Grund Care Centre', sub='Extension and conversion of a care centre to MINERGIE standard, competition 1st place',
  text='Extension, conversion and refurbishment of a care centre with home-care services (Spitex) and a dementia unit, MINERGIE-certified. Won in a selective competition.',
  desc=[['', 'One of the main tasks of the competition was to integrate the dementia unit into the care centre complex. So as not to overlay the urban ensemble of the “Heime im Grund” with additional buildings, the aim was to keep the extension – and with it the intervention in the parkland – as small as possible and to preserve the park and its trees. Embedded in the parkland, the secure unit on the garden level was extended with direct access to the outdoors. In this way the new building merges seamlessly with the existing structures of the old building, creating synergies that can be put to use. At the same time, the roof of the new unit forms an extended outdoor area for the cafeteria on the ground floor.'],
        ['', 'The extension is a compact addition to the existing head building of the care wing. On the standard floors, the additional meeting areas required more space. The communal areas – dining room with kitchenette, lounge and balcony – face south towards the park, creating a visual link with the outdoors. At the same time, the four-bed rooms on every floor were converted into single and double rooms with their own bathrooms. The entire care centre was refurbished and certified to the MINERGIE standard (ZH-1900). On the ground floor, the extension also enlarges the multi-purpose hall with the cafeteria. It also creates a direct relationship with the surroundings and, thanks to its position, reinforces the urban, public character of the cafeteria in relation to the parkland.']])

P('266-sku', name='Krämeracker School', sub='Conversion and refurbishment of the school',
  text='Krämeracker School, built in 1957, was in urgent need of refurbishment within its existing building envelope, which was still in good condition.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'Krämeracker School, built in 1957, was in urgent need of refurbishment within its existing building envelope, which was still in good condition. Over the previous ten years, individual components such as the windows and the heating system had already been replaced. The refurbishment now completed mainly renewed the building services and refreshed all surfaces.'],
        ['Layout', 'The wish for a larger staff room was met by relocating the adjacent materials room. The staff room now combines a large work area with a seating area and kitchenette.'],
        ['', 'Electrical installations and lighting: the electrical installations throughout the building were outdated in terms of safety, ease of use and IT connectivity, and were completely replaced. The lighting in the classrooms no longer met today’s need for flexible teaching and was renewed with suspended direct/indirect louvre luminaires, improving illuminance, freedom from glare and the experience of the space.'],
        ['Fittings', 'In the classrooms, the surfaces were thoroughly refurbished, and board rails with mobile boards and map holders were newly installed. The cabinets were refreshed and redesigned as pinboards.'],
        ['Grounds', 'The central playground was resurfaced with asphalt and enhanced with a “tree island” with seating.']])

P('270-bsb', name='SBB Railway Station & Shop', sub='New timber building to MINERGIE level with pellet heating',
  text='New railway station building with an avec shop, in timber construction to MINERGIE level, with pellet heating.',
  desc=[['', 'The new SBB office in Bonstetten was built in timber right next to and parallel with the tracks. The building is a long, simple block with canopies attached along the passenger area.'],
        ['', 'The brief comprises the SBB premises with ticket hall, offices, a staff room and storage. Separated by a toilet area for customers and staff, the other half of the building is used by an avec shop with its own storage and plant rooms.'],
        ['', 'The façades are characterised by large areas of glazing with floor-to-ceiling sliding doors, making the interior highly transparent. The closed façade elements are clad with rear-ventilated expanded metal, giving the building a character that is both technical and refined. In contrast to the exterior, which no longer reveals the timber construction, the interior surfaces consist mainly of raw, painted OSB panels.'],
        ['', 'The building was constructed to the MINERGIE standard and has wood pellet heating and ventilation with heat recovery.']])

P('275-bsaf', name='SBB Railway Station & Shop', sub='New suburban railway station with convenience shop to MINERGIE level',
  text='New suburban railway (S-Bahn) station with a large platform canopy and a convenience shop to MINERGIE level in Zurich Affoltern.')

P('278-seh', name='Im Eich School', sub='Conversion with attic extension and energy-efficiency refurbishment',
  text='Conversion and complete energy-efficiency refurbishment of a primary school with eight classrooms, a staff room and a multi-purpose room.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The primary school designed by Max Ziegler and built in 1963 was badly worn and had fundamental problems with earthquake safety and the stability of its floor slabs. Given the generous construction and the spare space in the unconverted attic, a conversion combined with a complete refurbishment was chosen.'],
        ['Appearance', 'The refurbishment of the façades and windows left the external appearance entirely unchanged. Only the roof was given four generous dormers on the south side. The staircases are extensively glazed, creating light-filled stairwells and corridors.'],
        ['Layout', 'The school now has six classrooms, a craft room, a flexible room, a staff room and a generous multi-purpose room in the attic. Each classroom has its own group room, which the users value highly. The usual ancillary rooms complete the accommodation. In the basement, a pupils’ library was created by making use of a corridor area.'],
        ['Structural measures', 'Installing a lift ensured earthquake safety, and bonded carbon-fibre (CFRP) reinforcement took care of the deflection of the floor slabs. The slender steel columns were strengthened with half-shells and given a fire-protective coating, preserving the delicate overall impression.'],
        ['Building services', 'The electrical, heating and plumbing installations were completely renewed. Particular attention was paid to the lighting design in the classrooms and corridors.'],
        ['Fittings and colour concept', 'Most of the classrooms were newly furnished. The colour concept distinguishes between the different uses: classrooms, group rooms and corridors. The acoustic ceilings are uniformly white.']])

P('281-mzv', name='Zürcherstrasse Development', sub='New development with buildings for housing, offices, commerce and a day nursery',
  text='Two new apartment buildings, a new commercial building, and the complete gutting and conversion of a 19th-century building protected as part of the townscape.',
  desc=[['', 'The development on Zürcherstrasse/Tolackerstrasse in Volketswil consists of a listed 19th-century residential building, an extension with two apartment buildings, and a commercial building. All four buildings can be reached from the new underground car park.'],
        ['Appearance', 'Studies of possible uses showed how the semi-detached house with its neoclassical façade could be converted for commercial and residential use. Gutting the building made it possible to keep the external façade. Inside, three new floors were created for a day nursery, accessed via an internal staircase that also provides the required earthquake resistance.'],
        ['Apartment mix', 'In the second row, two apartment buildings with four 3.5- to 4.5-room apartments and two maisonettes of 3.5 and 5.5 rooms were built, accessed via an external staircase and a lift. Because of their size, the two cubes fit into the grain of the existing core-zone neighbourhood.'],
        ['', 'On Tolackerstrasse stands the commercial building with a double-height workshop, an office floor and a penthouse apartment. The whole development is accessed from Tolackerstrasse.'],
        ['Heritage inventory', 'The theme of the listed building in the core zone – a clearly structured façade with glazing bars and hinged shutters – is continued in the residential buildings with sliding shutters in the window reveals and in the commercial building with sliding shutters mounted in front of the façade, so that the new buildings fit into the townscape in a contemporary way.'],
        ['Energy', 'The commercial building and the two apartment buildings are insulated to an above-average standard in order to meet future requirements too. The former residential building with its preserved neoclassical façade was given internal insulation.']])

P('289-mtf', name='Teienstrasse Terraced Building', sub='New terraced building with ideal views of Lake Zurich',
  text='New terraced building with three owner-occupied apartments and superb views of Lake Zurich.',
  desc=[['', 'On the edge of the residential area, surrounded by vineyards, the terraced building on Teienstrasse in Feldmeilen comprises three owner-occupied apartments and an underground level with a parking garage and ancillary rooms.'],
        ['Apartments', 'The single-storey apartments of up to 150 m² living space have large roof terraces of 70 m² looking towards Lake Zurich. Each apartment has its own private entrance from outside and is served centrally by a lift on the hillside.'],
        ['Energy', 'In line with the MINERGIE standard aimed for, the apartments are heated with geothermal energy via a ground probe.']])

P('292-mlw', name='Lediweg Terraced Building', sub='New terraced building with exclusive owner-occupied apartments',
  text='New terraced building with seven exclusive owner-occupied apartments to MINERGIE level, in an exceptional location with views in Uster-Wermatswil.',
  desc=[['', 'The new “Blickpunkt Lediweg” development is located on the sunny hill of Wermatswil, on the north-eastern edge of Uster. Surrounded by detached houses and apartment buildings, the terraced building blends into its surroundings and nestles into the slope. The seven owner-occupied apartments, all facing south-west, are accessed from Hanfroosenweg and via an underground car park with 15 spaces.'],
        ['', 'On four residential levels, the garden, ground and upper floors each contain two generous, individual 3.5- to 5.5-room apartments with living areas of 130 m² to 170 m², each with a terrace opening directly onto the garden or a roof terrace. The attic floor is reserved for a single apartment of 190 m² with roof terraces totalling 150 m².'],
        ['', 'The apartments are reached via a staircase and lift on the hillside within the building. The main entrance is at street level from Hanfroosenweg on the ground floor. The very deep building is structured by two light wells, which create accents of light in the entrance and living areas of the apartments and in the common circulation areas. Visible incisions in the west façade also create zones and separations between the individual apartments.'],
        ['', 'Staggered and stepped towards the south-west, the building appears as a solid block that opens up transparently to the sunny side with large areas of glass. Two window types define its monolithic appearance: large, floor-to-ceiling windows opening directly onto the roof terrace, and narrow, horizontal windows lighting and ventilating the ancillary rooms.'],
        ['', 'Projecting roofs allow covered terraces to alternate with open roof terraces. On the garden and ground floors, the slope means that the apartments open directly onto the outdoor space.']])

P('299-bssc', name='SBB Railway Station', sub='Conversion and refurbishment of a listed station building',
  text='Conversion and refurbishment of the SBB office and sales premises in a listed station building (built 1922), carried out in phases while the station remained in operation.')

P('300-bswae', name='SBB Railway Station & Medical Practices', sub='Conversion and refurbishment of a listed station building',
  text='Conversion and refurbishment of a listed station building (built 1934) with medical practices and a convenience shop.',
  desc=[['', 'The station building of 1933/34 is protected as a cantonal heritage monument and had already been converted inside several times, in 1978 and 1998.'],
        ['', 'As before, the ground floor houses the SBB ticket office and travel agency. In the vacant office space on the ground floor, a convenience shop with its ancillary rooms has now been created.'],
        ['', 'The upper floor previously contained three apartments, which have now been converted into a medical practice and staff facilities for the ground-floor shops – while the SBB premises remained in continuous operation. On the track side of the building, alongside further ancillary rooms, there is an accessible public toilet.'],
        ['', 'Because of the heritage requirements, the exterior of the façade, built in the 1930s in the style of the “Neues Bauen” (Swiss modernism), could not be altered. As part of the refurbishment, however, the windows and hinged shutters were renewed both in energy terms and visually, and the existing external venetian blinds, which were not true to the style, were replaced by fabric awnings. Minor adjustments were made to the windows and doors on the ground floor. Following the redesign of the outdoor areas and platform canopies in 2013, the ground-floor façade on the track side will be refurbished in a second phase.'],
        ['', 'Inside, the historic staircase was preserved and has now been supplemented by a lift suitable for wheelchair users and goods. Numerous partition walls were adapted to the new floor plan geometry, and all surfaces – walls, ceilings and floors – were completely renewed. In the course of the conversion, the heating, plumbing and electrical distribution systems were also renewed where affected by the work.']])

P('304-sth', name='Tobel School Complex', sub='New school building in monolithic construction',
  text='New school building with a staff room and rooms for specialist teaching for the Tobel school complex, in monolithic construction.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The Tobel school unit consists of several buildings and, owing to a lack of space, needed an extension for specialist teaching rooms. Several options were examined to find out how the required space could be provided on the site, taking account of the existing structures, without costly temporary buildings and while the school remained in operation.'],
        ['Brief', 'In line with the requirements of the cantonal education department, rooms were needed for integrative support, block-time childcare and German as a second language, as well as offices for school social work and the school management. The situation in the existing staff room was also inadequate. To cover the shortage of space, the extension was planned right next to the existing school building.'],
        ['Appearance / massing', 'The cubic building fits calmly into the existing ensemble and deliberately defers to its architectural language. The buildings are linked by a slender, almost floating canopy.'],
        ['Construction', 'The building is of solid construction. The basement walls, the floor slabs and the lift shaft are in concrete. The external walls are highly insulating monolithic masonry. On the extensively glazed south side, reinforced concrete columns carry the loads.'],
        ['Materials / colour', 'Rubber granulate flooring was laid in the staircase and washrooms, and parquet in the rooms. Some of the ceilings are finished with Baswaphon acoustic plaster.'],
        ['Energy and building services', 'A new plant room with a combined air-to-water heat pump and an oil boiler for peak loads was installed in the existing school building. All four buildings are connected to it via a new local heating network.'],
        ['Playground extension', 'The extension took up part of the existing playground, which had to be rebuilt. The previously unused meadow on the eastern edge of the site was therefore incorporated into the playground concept and enhanced with an arena of granite blocks, various play equipment and a climbing course.'],
        ['Special features', 'In accordance with the Swiss Disability Discrimination Act, the extension was fitted with a passenger lift.']])

P('306-gzm', name='Municipal Building', sub='Extension, conversion and refurbishment of the municipal building',
  text='Extension, conversion and refurbishment of the municipal building in Maur, carried out in phases while it remained in use.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The municipal building is located in the village centre of Maur, directly adjoining the historic, protected core zone. It consists of two single-storey buildings, each with two attic floors – a main building from 1962 and an extension from 1982. The two buildings house the administration of the Municipality of Maur and, in the basement (1982), the fire brigade.'],
        ['Layout', 'The renewal and extension of the municipal building to improve the available space comprises three parts:'],
        ['', 'The existing building from 1962 – a landmark volume on Zürichstrasse (refurbishment and conversion). The additional storey – a lower, two-storey replacement building at the centre of the site (new build to MINERGIE standard). The extension – a three-storey head building on Zürichstrasse with a new entrance hall (new build to MINERGIE standard).'],
        ['', 'The parts of the building are linked without steps or barriers and are grouped around an inner courtyard. In their orientation and volume, the buildings pick up the grain of the adjoining core zone.'],
        ['Material concept', 'The coexistence of old and new is reflected in the materials of the façades. On the existing 1962 building, a rear-ventilated construction of horizontal panels is placed in front of the cavity masonry. This façade meets the refurbishment requirements and visually underlines the horizontality of the 1962 building, which is to be preserved. The new parts – the extension and the additional storey – are solid structures with punched-window façades. The two types of tall rectangular windows are arranged on a façade grid. The façade consists of cavity masonry with an outer leaf of, for example, white-slurried clinker brick.']])

P('308-mzu', name='Zentralstrasse Development', sub='New housing development with commercial units in the historic core zone',
  text='New development of apartment buildings in the historic core zone of Uster, with commercial units on the ground floor.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The site lies within the perimeter of the inventoried townscape of Kirchuster, which is of regional importance, and in the core zone of Uster’s historic centre, on Zentralstrasse between Brauereistrasse and the Aabach stream.'],
        ['', 'Two existing buildings were replaced and a third building was added to densify and extend the core zone. The project essentially consists of three pairs of buildings that weave themselves into their built surroundings, together form an ensemble and define new courtyards and street spaces. On the corner of Zentralstrasse and Brauereistrasse, an eaves-fronted building was replaced by one of similar size and format, but gable-fronted. Together with the neighbouring house, which is also gable-fronted, it strengthens this important urban situation and forms a new street frontage onto Zentralstrasse, which widens out at this point. Set slightly back, beside the Aabach, the farm building of the house at Zentralstrasse 17 was replaced by a residential building. Its geometry and eaves height were taken from the existing building; only the north façade now follows the course of the Aabach. The third building, on Brauereistrasse, completes the existing situation and forms a new ensemble with the existing and replaced buildings. Its large, elongated volume is comparable to the commercial buildings along the southern edge of the castle hill, but its form responds to the small scale of its immediate neighbours. With its roofs, the building, divided into two parts, picks up the grain of the surrounding development, while its differentiated geometry creates clear relationships with the existing buildings. Tapering towards the west, the building relates to the buildings along Zentralstrasse, while its north-facing gable façade defines an attractive courtyard. The façade on Brauereistrasse, set slightly back from its neighbour, anticipates the curve of the street that follows with a barely visible kink.']])

P('309-mpg', name='Pfisterhölzli Apartment Buildings', sub='Façade refurbishment with a large photovoltaic system',
  text='Façade refurbishment of two apartment buildings from the 1970s with rear-ventilated cladding and a large photovoltaic system.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The two Göhner buildings, Pfisterhölzli 54 + 56 and Hirzerenstrasse 396 + 397, belong to the housing estate built in Greifensee at the end of the 1960s.'],
        ['Façade refurbishment concept', 'The apartment buildings contain 86 apartments. The cost-driven refurbishment concept for the two buildings was to insulate the closed, windowless north and south façades, and to restore the structure of the east and west window façades with concrete repairs and a new protective concrete coating, protecting them from weather and environmental influences in the long term. At the same time, a colour concept had to be developed that took account of the many changes of surface and material, the unity of the two buildings and the different wishes of the community of owners.'],
        ['Construction', 'In choosing the cladding material for the insulated parts of the façade, economic criteria came first, followed by ecological ones. The choice fell on core-coated aluminium panels, a cost-effective and durable product that completes the rear-ventilated façade. The end wall of the closed south façade was fitted with a large photovoltaic system of custom-sized laminated glass modules. The external walls were insulated to the MINERGIE standard.'],
        ['', 'The complex load transfer of the new rear-ventilated façades was taken through the inner leaf of the existing core-insulated exposed concrete façade.']])

P('310-gws', name='Parish Centre', sub='Partial demolition of an 1803 farmhouse and reconstruction as a parish centre',
  text='Partial demolition of a farmhouse and barn dating from 1803 and reconstruction as a parish centre with a large hall behind the historic façade.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The “Zum Wiesenthal” parish house in Schwerzenbach looks back on an eventful history. Built in 1803, it was used as a farmhouse with a barn until the 1970s, but in the 1980s and 1990s it stood empty and fell into disrepair.'],
        ['Project', 'While the residential half of the house was completely renovated in 2000, the former farm section has now been rebuilt on the house-within-a-house principle.'],
        ['Appearance', 'The exterior of the stable and barn section is preserved (clinker brick façade and timber boarding) or replaced where necessary. A small annexe is rebuilt on its historic footprint, but clad in contemporary timber boarding.'],
        ['Brief', 'The ground floor contains the foyer, kitchen and ancillary rooms, the first floor teaching rooms, and above them a multi-storey hall with galleries.'],
        ['Fittings and materials', 'By retaining the timber structure, or replacing it only in part, the spatial experience of a barn is preserved.'],
        ['Energy', 'The building envelope is built to MINERGIE requirements. Heat is generated by a pellet boiler. The hall has ventilation with heat recovery.']])

P('311-sbh', name='Breiti School', sub='Conversion of gymnasium, school kitchen and ventilation plant to MINERGIE level',
  text='Conversion and façade refurbishment of a school building with a gymnasium, attic conversion with group rooms, and a school kitchen.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'Breiti School, originally built in 1936, has gone through several phases of conversion, extension and modernisation in its history. Besides the energy-efficiency refurbishment of the building envelope and services, the modernisation of the interiors and a careful increase in space through an attic conversion, the aim was to make the many-sectioned school appear as a single entity, inside and out. The entire refurbishment was carried out while lessons continued.'],
        ['Brief', 'The main focuses of the refurbishment inside the building were: the attic conversion including new access, the school kitchen including renewal of the ventilation system, the renovation of the gymnasium in accordance with the guidelines of the Swiss Council for Accident Prevention (BFU), the conversion of the changing areas including a new staircase, and the renewal of all corridors.'],
        ['Appearance', 'See “Situation”. Construction: the building is of solid construction (concrete slabs on masonry walls). The façades were insulated with rendered EPS boards. The rafters were filled with blown-in insulation and covered with over-rafter insulation for thermal protection.'],
        ['Materials', 'Quality, durability, care and maintenance were the criteria for choosing the new materials. The classrooms were given hardwood floors, while all other floor surfaces and the walls in the washrooms of the changing rooms were finished seamlessly. To achieve balanced acoustics, all ceilings now have suspended ceiling systems. While more neutral colours dominate in the classrooms, corridors and attic, rich colour accents were set in the seamless wall finishes of the gymnasium’s wet areas, which receive little daylight.'],
        ['Energy and building services', 'Energy-saving LED lights were installed throughout the school. The basement houses a large ventilation plant with heat recovery for the school hall, the changing and shower areas of the gymnasium and the school kitchen. As not all rooms are connected to the ventilation system, no MINERGIE certification was sought. However, all elements of the building envelope were renewed to the MINERGIE standard. On the listed gable façade of the gymnasium, this was achieved with internal insulation.']])

P('312-gok', name='Municipal Building', sub='Conversion, energy-efficiency refurbishment and internal reorganisation',
  text='Conversion and energy-efficiency façade refurbishment of a municipal building from the 1950s, with internal reorganisation of the departments.')

P('313-gbw', name='Municipal Building', sub='Conversion and complete interior refurbishment of the historic municipal building',
  text='Conversion and complete interior refurbishment of the historic municipal building from 1840 in the village centre of Wald.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The building, built as a school in 1840 and used by the municipal administration since 1915, is listed on the municipal inventory of buildings worthy of protection.'],
        ['', 'Although the structure and the load-bearing capacity of the floors were in a critical state before work began, and the building met neither accessibility nor fire safety regulations, preserving the historic fabric was the top priority.'],
        ['Layout', 'Each floor accommodates one department with a lot of public contact and an open counter area, and one less frequented department, sharing a common reception area. The attic conversion makes room for a larger meeting room as well as a staff room.'],
        ['Accessibility', 'With the installation of a lift, all floors of the municipal building are now accessible without barriers. In agreement with the townscape and heritage protection authorities, a step-free entrance was created next to the entrance steps.'],
        ['Fire safety', 'The offices with their shared reception area were designed as a single horizontal fire compartment and separated from the staircase and lift by generous fire-resistant glazing.'],
        ['Structure', 'The floor and roof structures could be preserved thanks to various additional structural measures. To improve earthquake safety, nail strips were applied to the timber beams. At the same time, the new concrete lift shaft forms a stable core for the building.'],
        ['Building services', 'A wood pellet boiler and a decentralised ventilation system with heat recovery were installed, and the entire electrical installation was renewed.'],
        ['Materials and colour concept', 'To do justice to the old fabric of the building, new elements were added with great care, taking their cue in material and design from what was found on site.']])

P('318-epu', name='Pündtstrasse House', sub='New town villa in monolithic construction, MINERGIE-P',
  text='New three-storey town villa in monolithic construction, built to the MINERGIE-P standard.')

P('319-sag', name='Aussergass School', sub='New school building in concrete-timber hybrid construction',
  text='New school building with a day nursery, kindergarten, day-care facilities, classrooms and school administration, in concrete-timber hybrid construction.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The existing Aussergass school complex consists of several school buildings and a kindergarten, and needed an extension because of a lack of space. The site lies in the zone for public buildings and borders on the core zone and the agricultural zone.'],
        ['Brief', 'The new building houses the school administration, classrooms, group rooms, after-school care and a kindergarten on the ground floor, allowing synergies between school and extended day care. Because of the municipality’s rapid growth, an additional storey was added just four years after the building was completed.'],
        ['Appearance', 'The new building is set at right angles to the existing school and completes the school grounds. A link building connects it directly to the existing school and, thanks to the lift in the new building, makes all floors of the existing school accessible. The elongated building is structured by recessed entrances and loggias. A shallow hipped roof without overhang was chosen.'],
        ['Construction', 'The load-bearing structure is a skeleton frame of concrete slabs on reinforced concrete columns with individual concrete shear walls for bracing. The rest of the building, in hybrid construction, consists of non-load-bearing plasterboard partitions and a building envelope of timber elements. The entire additional storey on the second floor and the roof are also made of prefabricated timber elements.'],
        ['Materials', 'The façade is clad with rear-ventilated vertical timber boarding and complemented by coloured elements (blinds, horizontal metal elements). Inside, the character of the spaces is defined by rubber granulate floors, timber acoustic ceilings and exposed concrete or plastered walls.'],
        ['Energy and building services', 'Wood chip heating. Separate ventilation systems for the kitchen and the second-floor classrooms. The building envelope meets an enhanced MINERGIE standard (without certification). A photovoltaic system was added on the roof after the additional storey was built.'],
        ['Special features', 'The additional storey was built while the building remained in use. The roof, its superstructures, glazed elements and the ventilation systems were put into temporary storage and then reinstalled.']])

P('320-ksp', name='Parish Centre', sub='Replacement of the existing parish building',
  text='Replacement building, constructed to MINERGIE standard, for the existing parish building with administrative offices and the parish flat.')

P('321-pbv', name='Parish Centre', sub='Additional storey on the existing parish centre',
  text='Additional storey, conversion and refurbishment of the parish centre, built in prefabricated timber elements on top of the existing building from 1994.')

P('324-kbg', name='Böschacher Kindergarten', sub='Extension, conversion and refurbishment of the existing building',
  text='Extension, conversion and refurbishment of a historic, listed school building from 1826 in Grüt near Gossau.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The originally single-storey school building from 1826 was given an additional storey with a teacher’s flat in 1877. In 1951 an unsympathetic toilet annexe was added at the north-east corner, and in 1987 a further flat was built into the attic. The wish for a second classroom and a general need for refurbishment led to the removal of the toilet annexe, a new extension on the east side and a complete refurbishment of the building.'],
        ['Brief', 'The ground and upper floors each contain a classroom lit from three sides. The associated group rooms are in the extension and the newly converted attic. A “music room” was created in the newly fitted-out basement, with its own step-free entrance from the street.'],
        ['Appearance', 'While the existing building was restored to its historically correct state in collaboration with the heritage conservation office, the new extension was deliberately designed as a counterpoint to the main building.'],
        ['Construction', 'The new building is a prefabricated timber structure with a rear-ventilated timber batten façade on a concrete slab. In the old building, with its rubble masonry walls and timber joist floors, dry rot meant that the basement ceiling had to be completely removed and replaced with a concrete slab. Steel columns, clad on the historic model, support the intermediate floors.'],
        ['Materials', 'On the outside, the existing building was repaired with a roughcast render and a mineral paint. Inside, a new floating floor with herringbone parquet and ceramic tiles was laid on the old timber joists. The original wood panelling, which was still in good condition, was repainted.'],
        ['Special features', 'The original front door had been altered and reduced in size in 1951 when the toilet annexe and canopy were built. Using old postcards, it was possible to reconstruct the original door: the sandstone cornice with brackets and lintel and the oak door with wrought-iron grilles once again form a fitting main entrance. The original windows in the staircase area were also reconstructed.']])

P('325-kjw', name='Jonastrasse Kindergarten', sub='Extension, conversion and refurbishment of the existing building',
  text='Extension, conversion and refurbishment of a kindergarten from the 1980s in the core zone of Wald.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The site lies in the core zone of Wald and is subject to cantonal townscape protection. Beneath the entire building is a large civil defence shelter.'],
        ['Brief', 'The existing kindergarten building from the 1980s was partly stripped back to its shell, refurbished and extended by a further kindergarten unit, with a new internal organisation. Besides two classrooms, group rooms, cloakrooms in the staircase and toilets on the ground floor, the building houses an office, storage, building services and a multi-purpose room in the attic.'],
        ['Appearance', 'The extension had to follow the external dimensions of the existing building. Thanks to the refurbishment of the old part, however, the building could be designed as a single entity. The existing pitched roof is extended in steps on the east side to create a covered outdoor seating area.'],
        ['Construction', 'The external walls of the new part are of insulating single-leaf masonry (49 cm). The floor slabs are in concrete. The roof structure consists of timber elements in the new part and a traditional purlin and rafter construction in the old part.'],
        ['Materials', 'The rendered external walls fit into the core zone in colour and texture. The large-format wood-metal windows and the vertical awnings, however, reveal a contemporary construction. This also shows in the perforated covers of the window casements, laser-cut from HPL panels.'],
        ['Energy and building services', 'Heating and hot water are provided by a geothermal probe. The entire roof is covered with a fully integrated photovoltaic system.']])

P('326-shu', name='Hasenbühl School', sub='Conversion and refurbishment of the primary school in phases',
  text='Conversion and refurbishment of the primary school from 1913, protected by the canton, in several phases.')

P('327-sdu', name='Dorf Music School', sub='Conversion and refurbishment of a primary school into a music school',
  text='Conversion and refurbishment of a primary school from 1890, protected by the municipality, into a music school.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The former “Dorf” primary school of 1890 lies within the cantonal townscape protection zone in Uster and is listed on the municipal inventory of buildings worthy of protection. The building was last refurbished in the 1980s and its historic fabric is well preserved. Originally, only a temporary conversion for the music school was planned. Thanks to the successful redesign of the rooms, the good central location and the building’s “charm”, the temporary solution became permanent.'],
        ['Brief', 'Lightweight partitions with high sound insulation were installed in the large classrooms to create several small to medium-sized practice rooms for music lessons. Two large rooms on the second floor and in the basement serve as ensemble rehearsal rooms and can also be used for small performances. The staff room is in the attic.'],
        ['Appearance', 'The façade is characterised by timber windows with sandstone reveals. The individual floors are clearly marked by horizontal cornices. The roof is a hipped roof with cross gables. On the east and west sides, the main entrances are emphasised by a central projection and large flights of steps.'],
        ['Construction', 'The existing masonry walls carry concrete floor slabs, which had already been strengthened during earlier conversions. The new lightweight partitions are acoustically optimised, with double-stud construction.'],
        ['Materials', 'In close consultation with the heritage conservation office, the colours in the staircase were recreated on the historic model on the basis of paint layer analyses. The existing oak herringbone floors were restored and oiled.'],
        ['Building services', 'For wheelchair access and to transport musical instruments, a lift shaft was placed inside the building, serving all five floors and reached via a small side entrance.'],
        ['Special features', 'Soundproof drum booths were installed in two former group rooms as modular room-within-a-room solutions. All music rooms have movable curtains for individual adjustment of the reverberation time.']])

P('329-sam', name='Aesch School Complex', sub='Extension of an existing primary school complex',
  text='Extension and conversion of a primary school complex from the 1970s with an additional wing.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'Wing E is an extension of the existing Aesch school complex, which comprises several classroom wings and a gymnasium with a multi-purpose hall. The extension is placed in the southern corner of the site and connected to the other wings by a canopy.'],
        ['Brief', 'The building houses two classrooms and two group rooms with their ancillary rooms. Outside, the playground was adapted to the new situation, and new play equipment and climbing frames were installed in the garden.'],
        ['Appearance', 'The building is a simple cube set on the same alignment as the neighbouring wing D. There are already plans to renew wing D or add a storey to it, using the gap between the two wings as a “working joint” for the future construction work. A lift is also to be built into the gap between the buildings, so that it can be used by both parts of the building.'],
        ['Construction', 'The building is of solid construction, with concrete floor slabs and external walls of highly insulating single-leaf masonry (49 cm). Concrete walls with XPS insulation were used only for earthquake resistance and for the window parapets in contact with the ground on the hillside.'],
        ['Materials', 'The single-leaf masonry is rendered inside and out and matches the existing ensemble in texture and colour. Restrained natural colours for the fire doors, cloakrooms in solid oak and calming shades of green complete the look.'],
        ['Energy and building services', 'The building is connected via a district heating pipe to the heating network of the school complex and neighbouring properties, whose plant is fired with wood chips. All rooms have controlled comfort ventilation. A large photovoltaic system is installed on the roof.'],
        ['Special features', 'The building is MINERGIE-P certified, with the registration number “ZH-769-P”.']])

P('333-kff', name='Flamingo Children’s Hospice', sub='New children’s hospice as a care facility',
  text='New building for Switzerland’s first children’s hospice, for children with life-limiting illnesses, close to Lake Greifensee.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The site lies right next to the Roman Catholic parish centre of St. Catherine of Siena in Fällanden, the Sunnetal retirement centre and Lätten Primary School, and was acquired under a building lease. It is part of the public zone and, adjoining a reserve zone, occupies an exceptionally attractive position on the edge of Fällanden. Close by are the Zwicky site, the village centre with Fällanden’s community centre, and Lake Greifensee.'],
        ['Brief', 'The Swiss Children’s Hospice Foundation (Stiftung Kinderhospiz Schweiz) is building a children’s hospice to provide professional support for children and young people with life-limiting illnesses and their families. The ground floor houses eight care rooms, a nurses’ station, a care bathroom and storerooms, as well as the catering area with a kitchen and a lounge and dining room. The first floor contains six guest rooms and two connectable rooms, a lounge area with a terrace, and a homework and play room. The second floor accommodates offices and meeting rooms, as well as a multi-purpose room for 50 people.'],
        ['Appearance', 'The three-storey building is a subtractive volume with incisions for two roof terraces. The individual windows are slightly offset from floor to floor. The incisions respond to the neighbouring buildings and show consideration for them.'],
        ['Construction', 'The building is of conventional construction, with a basement, lift shaft and floor slabs in concrete. The external walls are of concrete and masonry with an external thermal insulation composite system. To allow for future changes of use, the non-load-bearing internal walls remain flexible and can be removed.'],
        ['Materials', 'The thermal insulation composite system is finished with a mineral render. Around the wood-metal windows, rendered panels stand out from the rest of the render in texture and colour.'],
        ['Energy and building services', 'Geothermal probes are drilled on the site, and a heat pump supplies the heating circuit and hot water. Controlled ventilation is divided between three units. The building has wheelchair-accessible bathrooms, a care bathroom and a photovoltaic system with battery storage for power cuts.']])

P('334-sbg', name='Berg School Complex', sub='Conversion and refurbishment of an existing secondary school complex',
  text='Conversion and refurbishment of parts of the buildings, the building services and the grounds of a listed school complex from the 1950s and 1970s.')

P('338-ebw', name='Binzholzstrasse House', sub='Energy-efficiency refurbishment of a detached house',
  text='This detached house from the 1980s stands on the edge of the village of Wald in a two-storey residential zone.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'This detached house from the 1980s stands on the edge of the village of Wald in a two-storey residential zone.'],
        ['Construction and materials', 'The existing solid building has cavity walls with little core insulation. Because of the age of the windows, blinds and external render, and to save on heating costs, an additional layer of thermal insulation was applied to the façade. To do so, the existing external timber boarding was removed from the “warm” parts of the walls. The openings were fitted with new wood-metal windows and new external venetian blinds. New colours for the rendered surfaces and the woodwork gave the house a more contemporary appearance.'],
        ['Special features', 'The windows were replaced during the owners’ two-week holiday, without any structural alterations inside the house and without disturbance to its interior.']])

P('341-spu', name='Pünt School', sub='Conversion and refurbishment of the after-school care centre and caretaker’s flat',
  text='Conversion and refurbishment of the after-school care centre and the caretaker’s flat.')

P('344-miu', name='Industriestrasse Apartment Building', sub='Refurbishment of an apartment building',
  text='Refurbishment of an apartment building.')

P('347-gtv', name='Tolackerstrasse Commercial Building', sub='New workshop halls and offices in timber construction',
  text='New commercial building with two workshop halls and offices, built as a highly insulated timber structure – part of the Zürcherstrasse site development in Volketswil.',
  desc=[['Situation', 'The commercial building at Tolackerstrasse 2 is part of the Zürcherstrasse/Tolackerstrasse site development in Volketswil. It comprises a listed 19th-century residential building, an extension with two apartment blocks, a commercial building in solid construction, a commercial building in timber and an underground car park. The landscaping weaves together the different user groups and shapes the transition from the village core zone to the industrial zone.'],
        ['Use', 'The new building houses two halls for metalwork and for plumbing and utility construction, together with their offices. The workshop operations place particular demands on deliveries, operational processes and noise protection, as well as on increased structural and imposed loads.'],
        ['Construction', 'The workshop hall was built without a basement as a highly insulated timber structure. The offices and ancillary rooms adjoin the two halls directly. The design had to take account of the internal loads of metalworking, and the floor build-up was designed for special loads, including vehicle traffic.'],
        ['Design', 'The theme of the listed building in the core zone – a clearly structured façade with glazing bars and hinged shutters – is continued in the commercial building with sliding shutters mounted in front of the façade, so that the new buildings fit into the townscape in a contemporary way.'],
        ['Phasing', 'The site development is being carried out in phases. By the end of June 2026, phases 1 and 2, with a building volume of around 18,300 m³, had been completed, handed over and occupied. The refurbishment of the existing workshop hall and the replacement building at Zürcherstrasse 16 will follow in 2027/28.']])
